 of the lines.\n\n#### Construction  \n\nDefine the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&:\\; y = x,\\\\\nL_{2}&:\\; x + y = 3,\\\\\nL_{3}&:\\; x + y = 4 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) has slope \\(1\\); it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(m=0\\)), the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(m=\\infty\\)), nor the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(m=-1\\)). Hence \\(L_{1}\\) is sunny.  \n* \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) both have slope \\(-1\\); they are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore are **not** sunny.  \n\nThe three lines are distinct because they have different constant terms.\n\n#### Verification (point‑by‑point)\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|-----------------|----------------------|-------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(1+2 = 3\\) ✓ |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(2+1 = 3\\) ✓ |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(1+3 = 4\\) ✓ |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(3+1 = 4\\) ✓ |\n\nAll six points of \\(S\\) are covered, and exactly one of the three lines (\\(L_{1}\\)) is sunny.\n\nHence for \\(n=3\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k=1\\) sunny line exists. ∎\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nI have reviewed the solution to ensure:\n* All required points are listed and covered.\n* The definition of “sunny” is applied correctly (no parallelism to the three forbidden directions).\n* The construction uses three distinct lines, with precisely one sunny line.\n* The verification table checks each point explicitly.\nNo logical gaps or missing justifications remain.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find a