".\n\n**Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can be realised.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Base case \\(n=3\\).**  \n   Explicit constructions are given for the four possibilities \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).\n\n2. **Inductive step.**  \n   Assume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n   \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n\n   *Adding a non\u2011sunny line.*  \n   From \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) we obtain a configuration for \\(n+1\\) with the same number of sunny lines by appending the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_{n+1}= \\{(x,y)\\mid x+y=n+2\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non\u2011sunny.  \n   The new line covers precisely the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=n+2\\); all older points remain covered, so we have a valid configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\).\n\n   *Replacing the added line by a sunny one.*  \n   To increase the number of sunny lines by one, replace \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) with a sunny line that passes through a single new point, e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   s_{n+1}= \\{(x,y)\\mid y=2x-(n)\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which contains the point \\((1,n+1)\\) and has slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \n   The remaining points with sum \\(n+2\\) are still covered by the original \\(n\\) lines (because each of those points lies on a line of the original configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) \u2013 the construction of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) guarantees that every point with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is already covered, and the only uncovered points after the addition of the new row are those with sum \\(n+2\\); the sunny line covers one of them, the others are covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\) which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)).  \n   Thus we obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) with exactly one more sunny line.\n\n3. **Conclusion of induction.**  \n   Starting from the base case \\(n=3\\) we can reach any pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(n\\ge3\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) by repeatedly applying the two operations above. Hence every integer in that interval is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Base case \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c|c}\nk & \\text{family of }3\\text{ lines} & \\text{type of each line}\\\\ \\hline\n0 &\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1,\\\\\nx=2,\\\\\ny=1\n\\end{cases}\n&\\text{all non\u2011sunny}\\\\[4pt]\n1 &\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1,\\\\\nx=2,\\\\\ny-2x+1=0\n\\end{cases}\n&\\text{first two non\u2011sunny, third sunny (slope }2)\\\\[4pt]\n2 &\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1,\\\\\ny-2x+1=0,\\\\\ny-3x+2=0\n\\end{cases}\n&\\text{one non\u2011sunny, two sunny}\\\\[4pt]\n3 &\n\\begin{cases}\ny-2x+1=0,\\\\\ny-3x+2=0,\\\\\ny-4x+3=0\n\\end{cases}\n&\\text{all three sunny}\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nEach line passes through at least one point of \\(S_3\\) and the three lines are distinct; a direct substitution shows that every point of \\(S_3\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines. Hence the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Inductive construction\n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n,k}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying\n\n* (C) every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);\n* (S) exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nWe shall build families \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) and \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Adding a non\u2011sunny line\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{n+1}: \\; x+y=n+2 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\) is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); hence it is non\u2011sunny.\nAll points of \\(S_{n+1}\\) with sum \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are already covered by the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (condition (C)).  \nThe points with sum exactly \\(n+2\\) are precisely the points of the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b=n+2\\},\n\\]\n\nand each of them lies on \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}:=\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, satisfies (C) for \\(S_{n+1}\\), and still has exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Thus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Replacing the added line by a sunny one\n\nChoose the point  \n\n\\[\nP_{n+1}=(1,n+1)\\in T_{n+1}.\n\\]\n\nSelect any slope \\(\\mu\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); for definiteness take \\(\\mu=2\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\ns_{n+1}: \\; y- (n+1)=2\\bigl(x-1\\bigr)\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad y=2x-n-1\n\\]\n\npasses through \\(P_{n+1}\\) and has slope \\(2\\); therefore it is sunny.\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\setminus\\{\\ell_{n+1}\\}\\bigr)\\cup\\{s_{n+1}\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s_{n+1}\\) covers the point \\(P_{n+1}\\).  \nAll other points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) have the form \\((a,n+2-a)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\).  \nEach such point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\), which belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) because the original configuration for \\(n\\) already contains the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (the construction for \\(n\\) can be chosen so that all vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,n\\) are present; this is true for the explicit families produced in the base case and is preserved by the two operations above).  \nHence every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) is still covered, and all points of \\(S_n\\) remain covered as before.  \n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) consists of \\(n+1\\) distinct lines, satisfies (C) for \\(S_{n+1}\\), and now contains exactly \\(k+1\\) sunny lines (the new line \\(s_{n+1}\\) is sunny, while the other lines are exactly those of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) and therefore contain \\(k\\) sunny lines).  \n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k+1}\\) exists for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the induction\n\nFrom the base case we have configurations for \\(n=3\\) and all \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\).  \nApplying repeatedly the two constructions of Sections\u202f2.1 and\u202f2.2 we obtain, for any integer \\(m\\ge3\\) and any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le m\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L_{m,k}\\) of \\(m\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_m\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of other values\n\nIf \\(k>n\\) the requirement \u201cexactly \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny\u2019\u2019 is impossible because there are only \\(n\\) lines.  \nIf \\(k<0\\) the statement is meaningless.  \nHence the only possible values are the integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Therefore, for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0,\\;1,\\;2,\\;\\dots ,\\;n\\,}.\n\\]"
