ered, but (3,1) not). (3,2) not covered, (4,1) not covered, (1,4) not covered.\n\nWe have used 3 lines: y=x, y=x+1, y=-2x+5. All sunny.\n\nNow we need a fourth sunny line to cover the remaining points: (1,4),(3,1),(3,2),(4,1). Let's see if we can find a line that covers at least two of these. For example, line through (1,4) and (3,2) slope (2-4)/(3-1) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. So not that.\n\nLine through (1,4) and (4,1) slope (1-4)/(4-1) = -3/3 = -1, also not allowed. So any line connecting (1,4) with any point of the form (i, j) where i+j = 5 (since (1,4) sum=5) will have slope -1? Let's check: (1,4) and (2,3): slope (3-4)/(2-1) = -1, not allowed. (1,4) and (3,2): slope -1, not allowed. (1,4) and (4,1): slope -1, not allowed. So any line connecting (1,4) to any other point on the same diagonal (sum=5) has slope -1. So we cannot cover (1,4) together with any other point on that diagonal using a sunny line. So (1,4) must be covered by a line that also includes points not on that diagonal. For example, line through (1,4) and (3,1) slope (1-4)/(3-1) = -3/2 = -1.5, which is not -1, so allowed. Let's compute: slope = -3/2. Equation: y - 4 = -3/2 (x - 1) => y = -3/2 x + 4 + 3/2 = -3/2 x + 11/2. Does this line pass through (4,1)? Let's check: y = -3/2*4 + 11/2 = -6 + 5.5 = -0.5, not 1. So no.\n\nDoes it pass through (3,2)? y = -3/2*3 + 11/2 = -9/2 + 11/2 = 2/2 = 1, not 2. So no.\n\nThus line through (1,4) and (3,1) covers those two points. That's a sunny line (slope -3/2). So we can use that as the fourth line. That covers (1,4) and (3,1). Then left points: (3,2) and (4,1). But we have used all 4 lines already. So we need to cover (3,2) and (4,1) with one of the existing lines. Let's see if any of the existing lines already cover them.\n\n- y=x: passes through (3,3) not (3,2) or (4,1). So no.\n- y=x+1: passes through (3,4) not (3,2) or (4,1). So no.\n- y=-2x+5: passes through (3,-1) not (3,2) or (4,1). So no.\n- y = -3/2 x + 11/2: passes through (3,1) (we used) and (1,4).