asses through (1,2) and (2,3); not (1,3). L3 passes through (1,1) and (3,2); not (1,3). L4 passes through (2,2) and (4,1); not (1,3). So (1,3) uncovered.\n\n- (1,4): covered by L1.\n- (2,1): not covered. L1: (2,2) and (1,4); not (2,1). L2: (2,3) and (1,2); not (2,1). L3: (1,1) and (3,2); not (2,1). L4: (2,2) and (4,1); not (2,1). So (2,1) uncovered.\n\n- (2,2): covered by L1, L4.\n- (2,3): covered by L2.\n- (2,4): not needed (since 2+4=6 >5). So ignore.\n\n- (3,1): not covered. L1: no. L2: no. L3: (3,2) not (3,1). L4: no. So (3,1) uncovered.\n\n- (3,2): covered by L3.\n- (3,3): not needed (3+3=6 >5). So ignore.\n\n- (4,1): covered by L4.\n\nThus uncovered points: (1,3),(2,1),(3,1). So this set fails.\n\nMaybe we can adjust lines to cover those. Let's try to find a better set.\n\nWe need to cover (1,3),(2,1),(3,1). Perhaps we can assign lines differently. Let's try to design lines that each cover one of the points in the last diagonal and also cover these uncovered points.\n\nWe have points in the last diagonal: (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). We need four lines, each passing through one of those. Let's assign:\n\n- L_a through (1,4) and (2,1) maybe? Slope (1-4)/(2-1) = -3, which is sunny. So L_a: y = -3x + 7 (since (1,4): 4 = -3*1 + b => b=7). This line also passes through (2,1) (since -3*2+7=1). So L_a covers (1,4) and (2,1). Good.\n\n- L_b through (2,3) and (1,2) maybe? Slope (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. L_b: y = x + 1 (since (1,2) => 2 = 1 + b => b=1). This line also passes through (2,3). So L_b covers (2,3) and (1,2). Good.\n\n- L_c through (3,2) and (1,1) maybe? Slope (2-1)/(3-1) = 1/2 = 0.5, sunny. L_c: y = 0.5x + 0.5 (as before). This line also passes through (3,2). So L_c covers (3,2) and (1,1). Good.\n\n- L_d through (4,1) and (3,? ) maybe (3,? ) = (3,? ) = (3,? ) such that slope not forbidden. Let's try (4,1) and (2,? ) maybe (2,? ) = (2,2) slope (1-2)/(4-2) = -0.5, sunny. So L_d: y = -0.5x + 3 (as before). This line also passes through (2,2). So L_d covers (4,1) and 