' + (k+1),\n     \\]\n     i.e. the point lies on \\(L_{i}\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\n   \u2013 Together the \\(n-k\\) horizontals and the \\(k\\) sunny lines give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering every required point, with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=n\\).**  \n   \u2013 Suppose all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \n   \u2013 Because a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point.  \n   \u2013 The horizontal line \\(y=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,1),(2,1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   \u2013 Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to supply **exactly one** of these points each.  \n   \u2013 Consider the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\), which contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),(2,n-1),\\dots ,(n,1)\\).  \n   \u2013 Since a sunny line is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\), it also meets this diagonal in at most one point.  \n   \u2013 The two families \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) and \\(\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) are disjoint, so the \\(n\\) sunny lines would have to provide **two** distinct points on each line, which is impossible with only \\(n\\) lines.  \n   \u2013 Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\nCombining the upper bound and the explicit construction yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{1}:y=0,\\qquad \\ell_{2}:x=0,\\qquad \\ell_{3}:x+y=0 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Because \\(L\\) is not parallel to \\(\\ell_{1}\\) it meets each horizontal line \\(y=b\\) in at most one point; similarly it meets each vertical line \\(x=a\\) and each diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) in at most one point.\n\nConsequently the intersection of \\(L\\) with the triangular region \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) is a set of points whose\n\\(x\\)-coordinates are all distinct and whose \\(y\\)-coordinates are all distinct.\nHence the points of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) lie on a strictly decreasing (or increasing) sequence of rows.\nA simple counting argument shows that the length of such a sequence cannot exceed  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\((a_{1},b_{1}),\\dots ,(a_{t},b_{t})\\) are points of \\(L\\cap\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) with\n\\(a_{1}<a_{2}<\\dots <a_{t}\\), then \\(b_{i}=c-a_{i}\\) for some constant \\(c\\) (because the slope is non\u2011zero).  \nSince \\(a_{i}+b_{i}=c\\le n+1\\) and the \\(a_{i}\\) are distinct integers, we must have  \n\n\\[\nt\\le\\frac{c-1}{2}\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor\\) points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines of a configuration are sunny.  By the above bound the total number of points that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause of (1),\n\n\\[\nn\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   \\;<\\; \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\qquad (n\\ge3),\n\\]\n\nso (4) is strictly smaller than the required number \\(|\\mathcal{P}_{n}|\\).  Hence a configuration with\n\\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.  Consequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) satisfying (5).  We shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The non\u2011sunny part\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{j}:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(H_{j}\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, therefore non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines (6).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered region\n\nThe points not yet covered are those with \\(b\\in\\{n-k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nDefine a translation  \n\n\\[\na'=a,\\qquad b'=b-(n-k).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUnder (7) the uncovered points become  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{Q}_{k}= \\{(a',b')\\mid a',b'\\ge1,\\;a'+b'\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a triangular set of size \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{k}\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y' = -\\,\\frac{i}{\\,k+1-i\\,}\\,x' + (k+1).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIn the original coordinates (undoing (7)) this is  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = -\\,\\frac{i}{\\,k+1-i\\,}\\,x + n+1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\) is never \\(0\\), never undefined, and never \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\nNow take any \\((a',b')\\in\\mathcal{Q}_{k}\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni = b' - a' .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a',b'\\ge1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le k+1\\), we have \\(0\\le i\\le k-1\\).  Adding \\(1\\) we obtain an integer\n\\(i+1\\) with \\(1\\le i+1\\le k\\).  Substituting \\(i+1\\) for \\(i\\) in (9) gives\n\n\\[\nb' = -\\,\\frac{i+1}{\\,k-i\\,}\\,a' + (k+1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the equation of the line \\(L_{i+1}\\).  Hence \\((a',b')\\) lies on \\(L_{i+1}\\).\n\nThus **every** point of \\(\\mathcal{Q}_{k}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\).  Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines (10) together with the \\(n-k\\) horizontals (6) cover all points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{n}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a configuration with all \\(n\\) lines sunny exists.\n\n*Row\u202f1.*  The set \\(\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) points.  Because a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, each such line meets the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) in at most one point.  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines must supply exactly one point of row\u202f1 each.\n\n*Diagonal\u202f\\(x+y=n+1\\).*  This diagonal contains the \\(n\\) points \\(\\{(a,n+1-a)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\}\\).  A sunny line is not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it meets the diagonal in at most one point.  Consequently the same \\(n\\) sunny lines must also supply exactly one point of this diagonal each.\n\nBut the two sets of points are disjoint.  A single line cannot simultaneously provide a point of row\u202f1 **and** a point of the diagonal, because that would force the line to contain two points with slopes \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\) simultaneously, which is impossible.  Hence the \\(n\\) sunny lines cannot cover both families, a contradiction.  Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines does not exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFrom Sections\u202f1 and\u202f3 we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nSection\u202f2 shows that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable configuration exists.  \nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le n-1\\,}.\n\\]"
