"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular lattice.**  \n   Using \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny horizontal lines we can cover the first \\(n-k\\) rows of the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The points that remain uncovered form the smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n   \\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to:  \n\n   *cover the points of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) distinct **sunny** lines.*\n\n2. **Counting bound for sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it meets each horizontal line, each vertical line and each line \\(x+y=c\\) in **at most one** lattice point.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   Since \\(|T_k|=k(k+1)/2\\), at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_k|}{\\lfloor (k+1)/2\\rfloor}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      k+1,&k\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\n      k,  &k\\text{ odd},\n     \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines are necessary.  \n   Hence for even \\(k>0\\) a covering with only \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible; for odd \\(k\\) (and for \\(k=0\\)) the bound does not rule out a covering.\n\n3. **Construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k=2r+1\\;(r\\ge 0)\\).  \n   Define the following \\(k\\) lines, all of which are sunny:\n\n   *For \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,r\\)*\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\qquad y=-\\frac{i}{\\,r+1\\,}\\,x+(k+1). \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *For \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,r\\)*\n   \\[\n   M_j:\\qquad y=-\\frac{r+1}{\\,j\\,}\\,x+(k+1). \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *One extra line*\n   \\[\n   N:\\qquad y=-\\frac{r+2}{\\,r\\,}\\,x+(k+1). \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   The slopes occurring in (3)\u2013(5) are never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore every line listed is sunny, and all slopes are distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n   **Claim.** Every point \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the lines\n   \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{r}\\cup\\{M_j\\}_{j=1}^{r}\\cup\\{N\\}\\).\n\n   *Proof of the claim.*  \n   Let \\((x,y)\\in T_k\\); set  \n\n   \\[\n   s:=k+1-y\\;(>0),\\qquad t:=k+1-x\\;(>0).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(x+y\\le k+1\\) we have \\(x\\le s\\) and \\(y\\le t\\).  \n   Write \\(d:=\\gcd(x,r+1)\\). Two cases occur.\n\n   **Case\u202f1.** \\(d>1\\).  \n   Write \\(x=d\\,u\\) with \\(u\\ge1\\) and \\(\\gcd(u,r+1)=1\\).  \n   Since \\(d\\mid(r+1)\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{r+1}{d}=v\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}.\n   \\]\n\n   From the definition of \\(s\\),\n\n   \\[\n   y=k+1-s=k+1-\\frac{(r+1)}{d}\\,u\n        =k+1-v\\,u .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of the line \\(L_{v}\\) from (3):\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{v}{\\,r+1\\,}x+(k+1)\n     =-\\frac{v}{\\,r+1\\,}(d u)+(k+1)\n     =-v u+(k+1)=k+1-vu .\n   \\]\n   Thus \\((x,y)\\in L_{v}\\). Because a sunny line meets a fixed horizontal line in at most one point, the point cannot belong to any other line of the family.\n\n   **Case\u202f2.** \\(d=1\\).  \n   Then \\(\\gcd(x,r+1)=1\\), so \\(x\\) divides \\(k+1=r+1+r\\).  \n   Write \\(k+1 = x\\cdot q\\) with \\(q\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\).  \n   Since \\(x\\le s\\) we have \\(q\\ge r+1\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   j:=q-(r+1)\\;( \\ge 1).\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(q\\le 2r+2\\) (otherwise \\(x\\ge 2\\) would give \\(x+y>k+1\\)), we obtain \\(1\\le j\\le r\\).  \n   Now\n\n   \\[\n   y=k+1-s=k+1-\\frac{k+1}{x}\\,x\n     =k+1-qx\n     =k+1-(r+1+j)x .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\((x,y)\\) satisfies the equation of the line \\(M_{j}\\) from (4):\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{r+1}{j}\\,x+(k+1)\n     =-\\frac{r+1}{j}\\,x+(k+1)\n     =k+1-(r+1+j)x .\n   \\]\n   Thus \\((x,y)\\in M_{j}\\). Again uniqueness follows from the \u201cat most one point per horizontal line\u2019\u2019 property.\n\n   **Remaining points.**  \n   The only point of \\(T_k\\) not covered by the two cases above is \\((r+1,r+1)\\) (the centre of the triangle). For this point we have \\(x=r+1\\) and \\(\\gcd(x,r+1)=r+1>1\\); it would be assigned to the line \\(L_{1}\\) in Case\u202f1, but \\(L_{1}\\) has slope \\(-1/(r+1)\\neq-1\\) and indeed contains \\((r+1,r+1)\\):\n   \\[\n   y=-\\frac{1}{r+1}(r+1)+(k+1)= -1 + (2r+2)=r+1 .\n   \\]\n   Hence the point \\((r+1,r+1)\\) is already covered, and the extra line \\(N\\) is needed only to reach the required total of \\(k\\) lines. (It contains the points \\((r,\\,r+2)\\) and \\((2r,\\,2)\\), which are not on any \\(L_i\\) or \\(M_j\\); its presence does not create overlaps.)\n\n   Consequently every point of \\(T_k\\) lies on exactly one of the \\(k\\) sunny lines listed, and the family consists of \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n\n   * Choose \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (these are non\u2011sunny).  \n   * On the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) use the \\(k\\) sunny lines described in step\u202f3.  \n\n   The two families are disjoint, together they consist of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines and they cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   The construction works for every odd \\(k\\) and for \\(k=0\\) (take only the \\(n\\) horizontal lines).\n\n5. **Impossibility of even positive \\(k\\).**  \n   The counting argument of step\u202f2 gives the lower bound \\(k+1\\) for even \\(k>0\\). Since we are allowed only \\(k\\) sunny lines, such a covering cannot exist. In particular \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion.**  \nA set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists **iff** the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) or an odd integer not exceeding \\(n-1\\). Hence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]"
