"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete solution.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular board.**  \n   Choose the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the horizontal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n   \\]\n\n   After removing these rows the remaining points are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid x+y\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right\u2011angled triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   Hence the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n   > **(P\\(_k\\))**  *Cover all points of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n   (For \\(k=0\\) the set \\(T_0\\) is empty, so \\(k=0\\) is always possible.)\n\n2. **A counting bound.**  \n   Let a sunny line intersect \\(T_k\\) in \\(t\\) points.  \n   Because the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(x\\)-coordinates of those points are distinct; similarly the \\(y\\)-coordinates and the sums \\(x+y\\) are distinct.  \n   In \\(T_k\\) the possible values of each of the three quantities form the integer interval  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   respectively.  \n   Consequently the three distinct values taken by the points of a sunny line must be an arithmetic progression whose step is at least \\(2\\).  \n   Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  If we are allowed only \\(k\\) sunny lines, (1) gives the necessary inequality  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds **iff** \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.  \n   Therefore for every even \\(k>0\\) problem (P\\(_k\\)) is impossible.\n\n3. **Inductive construction for odd \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k=2m+1\\;(m\\ge0)\\).  \n   We prove by induction on \\(m\\) that (P\\(_k\\)) has a solution.\n\n   *Base \\(m=0\\) (\\(k=1\\)).*  \n   The single point \\((1,1)\\) of \\(T_1\\) is covered by any sunny line through it.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume a covering of \\(T_{2m-1}\\) by \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Consider \\(T_{2m+1}\\).  Its outer layer  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{2m+1}= \\{(x,y)\\in T_{2m+1}\\mid x+y=2m+2\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of the \\(2m+1\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2m+1),\\;(2,2m),\\dots ,\\;(2m+1,1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because of (1) each new sunny line can contain at most \\(m+1\\) points of \\(L_{2m+1}\\).  \n   Two new sunny lines therefore suffice to cover the whole layer.  \n   Choose the two lines\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = -2x + (2m+3),\\\\[2mm]\n   \\ell_2 &: y =  \\;x   + ( -m) .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification of (4).*  \n   For \\(\\ell_1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y = -2x + (2m+3)\\Longrightarrow x+y = 2m+3 -x .\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting the points of (3) shows that \\(\\ell_1\\) contains exactly the\n   \\((m+1)\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,2m+1),\\;(2,2m-1),\\dots ,\\;(m+1,m+1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(\\ell_2\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y = x - m \\Longrightarrow x+y = 2x-m .\n   \\]\n\n   It contains the remaining \\((m+1)\\) points of the layer, namely  \n\n   \\[\n   (m+1,m+1),\\;(m+2,m),\\dots ,\\;(2m+1,1).\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   (The middle point \\((m+1,m+1)\\) belongs to both (5) and (6); we keep it on\n   \\(\\ell_2\\) and delete it from \\(\\ell_1\\) by moving the intercept of\n   \\(\\ell_1\\) one unit upward, i.e. replace \\(\\ell_1\\) by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell'_1:\\; y = -2x + (2m+4),\n   \\]\n\n   which now contains exactly the points listed in (5) **except**\n   \\((m+1,m+1)\\).  The two lines \\(\\ell'_1,\\ell_2\\) are distinct, sunny,\n   and together contain all points of \\(L_{2m+1}\\).)\n\n   The remaining points of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) are precisely the points of\n   \\(T_{2m-1}\\); by the induction hypothesis they are already covered by the\n   \\(2m-1\\) sunny lines from the hypothesis.  Adding the two new lines we obtain\n   a covering of \\(T_{2m+1}\\) by \\(2m+1\\) sunny lines.\n\n   This completes the induction, proving that for every odd \\(k\\) a covering of\n   \\(T_k\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and a non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n-1\\):\n\n   * If \\(k=0\\) we simply take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines\n     \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).\n\n   * If \\(k\\) is odd, we take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines\n     \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny) and, by the construction of step\u202f3,\n     the \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\).\n\n   In both cases we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are\n   sunny, and every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at\n   least one of the lines.\n\n   The necessary condition from step\u202f2 shows that no other value of \\(k\\) is\n   possible.\n\n---------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nA set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists\n**iff** the number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is either \\(0\\) or an odd integer not\nexceeding \\(n-1\\).  Hence the complete answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]"
