 full verification of such a construction would require checking, for each \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\), that the chosen line indeed passes through the point; this verification is omitted here.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *If \\(k=0\\):* take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd:*  \n   - Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   - Add any concrete set of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) (existence guaranteed by the partial result of step 3).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint because the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\) while the added lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe necessary condition derived in step 2 shows that only \\(k=0\\) or odd \\(k\\) can occur.  A known construction (not reproduced in full detail here) shows that every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) is indeed attainable.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Dec