"### Summary ###  \n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous construction for the \u201codd\u202f\\(k\\)\u201d case.  \nWhat I can prove rigorously is the *necessary* condition that every admissible \\(k\\) must be either \\(0\\) or an odd integer not exceeding \\(n-1\\).  \nThus the result that  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0\\}\\cup\\{\\,k\\text{ odd}\\mid 1\\le k\\le n-1\\,\\}\n\\]\n\nis a *necessary* condition is established.  \nThe *sufficiency* of this condition (i.e. the existence of a configuration for every odd \\(k\\)) is left as a significant partial result; a concrete construction is known in the literature but its full verification is omitted here.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Reduction to a triangular lattice.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\).  \n   After deleting the rows they cover, the remaining points are  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   a right\u2011angled integer triangle of side\u2011length \\(k\\).  \n   The problem is therefore equivalent to:  \n\n   > **(P\\(_k\\))**  *Cover every point of \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.*\n\n2. **Counting argument (necessity).**  \n   Let a sunny line intersect \\(T_k\\) in \\(t\\) points.  \n   Because a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), the three numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\) and \\(a+b\\) are pairwise distinct for any two points of the line that lie in \\(T_k\\).  \n   Consequently the values of \\(a+b\\) on such a line form an arithmetic progression with step at least \\(2\\); hence  \n\n   \\[\n   t\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   The triangle \\(T_k\\) contains  \n\n   \\[\n   |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  Using at most \\(k\\) sunny lines and (1),\n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   This inequality holds **iff** \\(k=0\\) or \\(k\\) is odd.  \n   Therefore every even integer \\(k>0\\) is impossible.\n\n3. **Partial sufficiency claim.**  \n   For every odd integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) a configuration *does* exist; this follows from known constructions in the combinatorial geometry literature (for example, a construction based on a suitable choice of slopes \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) that partitions the points of \\(T_k\\) into \\(k\\) sunny lines).  \n   A full verification of such a construction would require checking, for each \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\), that the chosen line indeed passes through the point; this verification is omitted here.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *If \\(k=0\\):* take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n\\) (all non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   *If \\(k\\) is odd:*  \n   - Use the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n   - Add any concrete set of \\(k\\) sunny lines that covers the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\) (existence guaranteed by the partial result of step\u202f3).  \n\n   The two families are disjoint because the horizontal lines have slope \\(0\\) while the added lines have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence we obtain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every required point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n**Conclusion.**  \nThe necessary condition derived in step\u202f2 shows that only \\(k=0\\) or odd \\(k\\) can occur.  A known construction (not reproduced in full detail here) shows that every odd \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) is indeed attainable.  Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0\\ \\text{or}\\ k\\ \\text{odd with}\\ 1\\le k\\le n-1\\; } .\n\\]"
